  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>620</x>
      <y>960</y>
      <w>390</w>
      <h>140</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Watches each search folder recursively, including
subfolders created later, on its own watcher thread.
For each change it offers the path to ChangeQueue if
IndexConfig includes it; when the operating system
reports lost events (overflow), it offers the whole
search folder instead. A rename arrives as a delete
plus a create; ContentStore makes that cheap.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>1190</x>
      <y>140</y>
      <w>440</w>
      <h>140</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Thread-safe. offer() is called from the watcher thread and
records the latest change time for each path, so a burst of
events for one file becomes a single entry.
takeReady() is called by the indexing worker. It returns a path
only once it has been unchanged for quietPeriod (about 2 s),
or null when nothing is ready before the timeout.
A folder in the queue means "rescan this folder".</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>570</x>
      <y>1230</y>
      <w>500</w>
      <h>220</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Only started in WRITER mode. Its worker thread is the only
writer of SearchIndex and ContentStore. requestUpdate() only
offers a path to the queue, so it returns immediately and is
safe from any thread.
Phase 1, indexFile(), makes a file keyword-searchable. If
ContentStore already has its content, the stored chunks and
embeddings are copied instead of extracting again.
Phase 2, embedNextBatch(), embeds content that has no embeddings
yet when no file is ready, and prunes ContentStore when there
is nothing to embed.
Status listeners are called on the worker thread.
state takes the values of the states in SM01.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>1210</x>
      <y>590</y>
      <w>410</w>
      <h>190</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Detects the file type from the file's contents and
returns its text page by page, with its type and
metadata (an ExtractedDocument).
Must not be able to crash or hang the application:
parsing runs isolated from the app's process, with a
time limit and a memory limit.
Throws UnsupportedFormatException for formats it can't
read, an IOException if the file is locked, and
ExtractionException if the file is corrupt or
password-protected, or parsing hit a limit or crashed.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>630</x>
      <y>1780</y>
      <w>370</w>
      <h>100</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Adapts a document-parsing library that can run
in a separate process (for example, Apache Tika's
forked parser) to TextExtractor. The rest of the
system never sees the library's types.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
  <element>
    <id>UMLNote</id>
    <coordinates>
      <x>1270</x>
      <y>920</y>
      <w>280</w>
      <h>60</h>
    </coordinates>
    <panel_attributes>Splits each page separately, so every
chunk keeps its page number.</panel_attributes>
    <additional_attributes/>
  </element>
